# OMCR Queue Management System

A queue management system for the Office of the Municipal Civil Registrar (OMCR),
Rosario, Batangas. Plain HTML/CSS/JS — no build step, no framework, no npm
dependencies.

## Pages

| Page | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `index.html` | Public splash screen + "Get a Queue Number" flow |
| `birth-window1.html` | Staff page — Birth Certificate, Window 1 |
| `marriage-window2.html` | Staff page — Marriage Certificate, Window 2 |
| `death-window3.html` | Staff page — Death Certificate, Window 3 |
| `correction-window4.html` | Staff page — Correction of Entry, Window 4 |
| `tv-display.html` | Public queue board, meant for a lobby TV/monitor |

## Project structure

```
omcr-app/
├── index.html
├── birth-window1.html
├── marriage-window2.html
├── death-window3.html
├── correction-window4.html
├── tv-display.html
├── css/
│   ├── styles.css              (shared splash screen styles — distinct palette)
│   ├── base.css                (shared reset + palette for staff/TV pages)
│   └── <page>.css              (one per staff/display page — layout + --accent color)
├── js/
│   ├── app.js                  (splash screen + "Get a Queue Number" logic)
│   ├── config.js                (Supabase project URL + anon key)
│   ├── supabase-client.js       (shared data-access layer, see below)
│   └── <page>.js                (one per staff/display page)
├── images/
│   ├── rosario-seal.jpg
│   └── bagong-pilipinas.webp
└── supabase/
    └── schema.sql               (run once in your Supabase project)
```

## Running locally

No build step required.

1. Open the folder in VS Code.
2. Install the **Live Server** extension (by Ritwick Dey).
3. Right-click `index.html` → **Open with Live Server**.

Or just open any `.html` file directly in a browser — everything is
self-contained via relative paths (`css/`, `js/`, `images/`).

## Backend — Supabase

Data is stored in a **Supabase** Postgres project (previously Firebase
Realtime Database).

### Setup

1. Create a project at [supabase.com](https://supabase.com).
2. Open **SQL Editor → New query**, paste in the contents of
   `supabase/schema.sql`, and run it. This creates every table the app
   needs (`queue`, `service_counters`, `serving_numbers`, `active_windows`,
   `daily_counts`, `audit_log`, `backups`, `staff_roles`) plus role-scoped
   Row Level Security policies (the public kiosk/TV display can only read
   and create tickets; everything else requires a signed-in staff account).

   If you already ran an earlier version of this schema, see the
   migration notes near the bottom of `supabase/schema.sql` — there are
   a few `alter table`/policy statements to run instead of the whole file.
3. Open **Project Settings → API** and copy:
   - **Project URL** → paste into `SUPABASE_URL` in `js/config.js`
   - **anon public** key → paste into `SUPABASE_ANON_KEY` in `js/config.js`

> Like the Firebase Web API key before it, the Supabase **anon** key is
> safe to expose client-side by design — it's not a secret credential.
> Actual data access is controlled by the Row Level Security policies in
> `supabase/schema.sql`, not by hiding this value.

### How pages talk to Supabase

Every page loads `js/config.js` then `js/supabase-client.js` before its own
script. `supabase-client.js` provides `fbGet(path)` / `fbSet(path, data)` /
`fbUpdate(path, data)` — the same function names and call signatures the
pages already used against Firebase — so each page's own logic didn't need
to change, only what's underneath. It translates Firebase-style tree paths
(e.g. `queue/B001`, `counters`) into Supabase table queries.

## Staff login

The staff-facing pages (`birth-window1.html`, `marriage-window2.html`,
`death-window3.html`, `correction-window4.html`) require sign-in via
**Supabase Auth** (email/password).

### Creating a staff account

1. Supabase dashboard → **Authentication → Users → Add user**. Set an
   email and password.
2. Copy that user's **UID**.
3. In **SQL Editor**, assign their role:
   ```sql
   insert into staff_roles (user_id, role, display_name)
   values ('<uid>', 'birth', 'Fe Dimaculangan');
   ```
   `display_name` is optional — if left out, "Handled By" falls back to
   showing the account's email instead.
   Role must be one of: `birth`, `marriage`, `death`, `correction`, or
   `admin` (an `admin` account can sign into every window).

## Voiding a ticket

If a customer leaves before being called, staff can click the small
**×** button next to their ticket in "Up Next" to void it. Voided
tickets are automatically skipped when calling the next number —
no changes needed to the counters, they just get passed over.

## Known limitation

Ticket numbers are assigned with a read-then-increment (`fbGet('counters')`
→ `fbUpdate('counters', ...)`), not an atomic database operation. Two
people submitting at the exact same instant could theoretically be handed
the same number. This mirrors the original Firebase implementation's
behavior — worth hardening with a Postgres function
(`increment_and_return`) if this becomes a real-world issue under load.